<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bóbr</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bober</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bóbr, in German Bober, is a river in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, flowing about 270 kilometres from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudetes">Sudetes</a> north through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-silesia">Lower Silesia</a> to join the Oder. Jelenia Góra, Bolesławiec and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagan">Żagań</a> lie on its banks, and it joins the Oder at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krosno-odrzanskie">Krosno Odrzańskie</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bobr.jpg" alt="Bóbr" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Platanacero · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Janowice_Wielkie,_Bobr_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Bóbr, called Bober in German, takes its Polish name from the word for beaver. It is a left tributary of the Oder. It rises on a slope of the Rýchory mountains in the south-east of the Krkonoše range, near the village of Bobr in the Czech Hradec Králové Region, and has a basin of 5,874 square kilometres, almost all of it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. It runs north-westwards through the Jelenia Góra valley of the Western Sudetes to the dam of Pilchowice and then down into the plains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-silesia">Lower Silesia</a>, passing Jelenia Góra, Bolesławiec, Szprotawa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagan">Żagań</a>, where the Kwisa joins it, and reaches the Oder near Krosno.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the lower Bóbr, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagan">Żagań</a> and the confluence with the Kwisa, marked the border between the historic regions of Silesia in the east and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-lusatia">Lower Lusatia</a> in the west. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gero-the-great">Margrave Gero</a> died in 965, the river became the eastern border of the new March of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. Emperor Otto III acknowledged Polish sovereignty under Duke Bolesław I Chrobry at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-gniezno">Congress of Gniezno</a> in 1000. Bolesław claimed the lands west of the Bóbr and held them for a time under the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bautzen">Bautzen</a> of 1018, but Emperor Conrad II had reconquered the territory by 1031.</p><p>The river became an internal border when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> brought the Piast dukes of Silesia under his rule, a process confirmed by the Treaty of Trentschin in 1335, after which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a> and Silesia both became lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lands-of-the-bohemian-crown">Bohemian Crown</a>. At the Potsdam Conference in 1945 the Western Allies briefly advocated a Polish-German border along the Oder, Bóbr and Kwisa, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a> rejected it, having already committed himself to the Oder-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nysa">Neisse</a> line.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Pilchowice Dam, built between 1904 and 1912 near Jelenia Góra and then the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, created a reservoir about four kilometres long. Its masonry stands on a gneiss base built on the Intze principle, and its hydroelectric plant has a rating of 7,585 kilowatts. Parts of the valley are protected, among them the Rudawy Landscape Park, the Bóbr Valley Landscape Park and the Przemków Landscape Park, and the source lies within the Krkonoše National Park.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bóbr” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It served the conversion of the Sorbs and the Slavic lands between the Saale and the <b>Bóbr</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-meissen">Prince-Bishopric of Meissen</a></li></ul></section>
